"A package diagram describes namespace organization and package-level relationships rather than runtime communication.\n\nUse when: Explain how model elements are organized into namespaces. Review package imports, merges, and dependency direction.\n\nAvoid when: A source import inventory or deployment topology is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Tabbed package shapes denote namespaces and optional nested packages. Dashed arrows show dependencies or explicitly stereotyped imports and merges. Qualified names disambiguate elements belonging to different packages.\n\nBuild: Identify the model scope and the package naming convention. Place nested packages inside padded parent boundaries. Add only relationships relevant to namespace visibility or model composition. Route long dependencies around package headers and label their stereotypes concisely.\n\nCheck: An import is not mislabeled as package containment. Merge semantics are used only when the models actually combine. Package names correspond to the stated namespace view. Nested boundaries leave enough space for both titles and connectors.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming package nesting implies runtime ownership or deployment. Drawing every element inside each package and obscuring package relationships.\n\nExample: Commerce imports common Money types from SharedTypes while Fulfillment keeps its own Shipment namespace.\n\nAlternatives: Module dependency graph, Class diagram."
